In `dev::setup`, the thread spawned to check mismatched packages uses a non-`move` closure that reads `dirs.frontend` and `dirs.tauri`. `dirs` is a `&Dirs` parameter, so the closure borrows data that isn't `'static`, and `std::thread::spawn` requires `'static`. Before this PR both values were `&'static` globals, so the closure was fine. Now it should fail with E0373 or E0521. I could not run `cargo check` here to confirm.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-cli/src/dev.rs" lines="138-148"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The fix is to copy the two `&'static Path` fields into locals and use `move`. Copying them is free because they are `'static`.

::suggestion{file="crates/tauri-cli/src/dev.rs" lines="144-148"}
let (frontend, tauri) = (dirs.frontend, dirs.tauri);
std::thread::spawn(move || {
  if let Err(error) = check_mismatched_packages(frontend, tauri) {
    log::error!("{error}");
  }
});

`mobile/ios/mod.rs::get_config` used to set `features` to `None` when there were no features, and `Some(features)` otherwise. The PR replaces that with an unconditional `Some(ios_options.features)`. This is unrelated to the statics refactor and changes the `ApplePlatform` values passed to cargo_mobile2 on every iOS invocation, even when no features are set. Depending on how cargo_mobile2 serialises an empty `Some(vec![])`, the generated project or build arguments may change.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-cli/src/mobile/ios/mod.rs" lines="268-276"}

::callout{type="warning"}
If the change is not intentional, restore the original conditional. If it is intentional, call it out in the PR description and in the `.changes` entry.

Do not change the default silently inside a refactor.

`android/build.rs::run` takes `let tauri_config = &tauri_config.lock().unwrap();`. The guard therefore lives until `run` returns, covering `set_current_dir`, `ensure_init`, `crate::build::setup`, and `run_build`. None of those lock the config again, so this doesn't deadlock today. But the build path only reads the config, so the lock adds nothing here. It also means any future code that locks the config during a build will deadlock silently.

::code{file="crates/tauri-cli/src/mobile/android/build.rs" lines="150-156"}

Prefer having `command` and `run.rs` hold the Mutex only where the watcher needs it, and passing `&ConfigMetadata` into `run`.

`ios/run.rs::command` calls `build::command`, and `build::command` loads its own `ConfigMetadata`. `run.rs` then loads a second copy with the same `--config` overrides so it can hand a `Mutex` to `watch`. The two loads read `tauri.conf.json` at different times, so the build and the watcher can disagree if the file changes between them. It also doubles the parse and schema validation work.

::code{file="crates/tauri-cli/src/mobile/ios/run.rs" lines="98-108"}

If `build::command` exposed its `ConfigMetadata`, `run.rs` could reuse it and skip the second load.

The PR is titled "Less statics", but `Dirs` holds `&'static Path` values that point into the `FRONTEND_DIR` and `TAURI_DIR` `OnceLock`s. That forces the globals to remain, and it is also why the spawn in `dev.rs` is tempting to write with a borrow. Storing owned `PathBuf`s in `Dirs` (or an `Arc<Path>`) would remove the need for `OnceLock` entirely. The cost is a clone per command, which is negligible here.

::code{file="crates/tauri-cli/src/helpers/app_paths.rs" lines="24-33"}

This is a design trade-off, not a bug. It's worth deciding now, while the API is being reshaped.

The new `reload_config` builds the replacement `ConfigMetadata` fully before assigning it with `*config = load_config(...)?`. A failed reload therefore leaves the previous config in place, which matches the old behaviour where `reload` only replaced the global on success. The watcher only swaps in the new manifest when reload succeeds. This removes roughly twenty `lock().unwrap().as_ref().unwrap()` panic sites that the old global handle needed, and replaces the "config not loaded" bail paths with ownership that cannot be missing.

::code{file="crates/tauri-cli/src/helpers/config.rs" lines="246-252"}

This is a good change.
